---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';

const base = `${import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '')}/`;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Compare Android browsers — Solipsism Browser"
  description="A sourced comparison of Solipsism Browser with Firefox, Brave, Cromite, and Vivaldi on Android, checked 31 October 2026."
  path="/compare/"
>
  <Header />
  <main id="main">
    <section class="archive-hero shell reference-hero">
      <p class="kicker">A practical comparison · checked 31 October 2026</p>
      <h1>Choose the browser<br /><em>that fits your day.</em></h1>
      <p>
        These projects take different approaches to Android browsing. This is a
        source-linked feature overview, not a benchmark or a ranking. Product
        details can change; follow each project’s current documentation before
        choosing or installing a browser.
      </p>
      <div class="page-actions">
        <a
          class="button"
          href="https://github.com/Kenneth-Cho-InfoSec/Solipsism/releases/latest"
          >Get Solipsism</a
        >
        <a class="button button-secondary" href={`${base}features/`}
          >See its features</a
        >
      </div>
    </section>

    <section
      class="section shell comparison-section"
      aria-labelledby="comparison-heading"
    >
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="kicker">Different priorities</p>
        <h2 id="comparison-heading">The browser is part of the choice.</h2>
        <p>
          Compare the core, controls, extensibility, and practical boundaries
          each project documents.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div
        class="comparison-table-wrap"
        role="region"
        aria-label="Android browser comparison table"
        tabindex="0"
      >
        <table class="comparison-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Browser</th>
              <th scope="col">Core and availability</th>
              <th scope="col">What its documentation emphasizes</th>
              <th scope="col">Trade-offs to consider</th>
              <th scope="col">Source and license</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Solipsism</th>
              <td
                >Android WebView is the stable core. Solipsism 9.4.0 also offers
                the optional Antares 0.5.0 core as experimental.</td
              >
              <td
                >Compact left or right navigation rail, privacy settings, host
                and cosmetic filter subsets, userscripts, and local download
                scanning.</td
              >
              <td
                >Android-only. Web support depends on the device’s WebView
                provider. Some permission controls are best-effort; Antares is
                experimental, and userscripts and filters are not full desktop
                equivalents.</td
              >
              <td
                ><a href="https://github.com/Kenneth-Cho-InfoSec/Solipsism"
                  >Project repository</a
                ><br /><a
                  href="https://github.com/Kenneth-Cho-InfoSec/Solipsism/blob/main/LICENSE"
                  >MPL 2.0</a
                ></td
              >
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Firefox for Android</th>
              <td
                >Firefox for Android, distributed by Mozilla; add-ons are
                installed from its supported add-ons catalog.</td
              >
              <td
                >Enhanced Tracking Protection and a curated Android add-on
                catalog, with add-on availability documented by Mozilla.</td
              >
              <td
                >Check whether the extensions you rely on are available for
                Android; the mobile catalog is not the entire desktop add-on
                ecosystem.</td
              >
              <td
                ><a
                  href="https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/enhanced-tracking-protection-firefox-android"
                  >Tracking protection</a
                ><br /><a
                  href="https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/find-and-install-add-ons-firefox-android"
                  >Android add-ons</a
                ><br /><a href="https://www.mozilla.org/en-US/MPL/">MPL 2.0</a
                ></td
              >
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Brave</th>
              <td
                >Brave Browser for Android uses Chromium and is distributed by
                Brave.</td
              >
              <td
                >Shields provides built-in controls for trackers, ads, cookies,
                and related page protections, with per-site behavior documented
                by Brave.</td
              >
              <td
                >Shields defaults and compatibility choices may need adjustment
                for individual sites. Brave’s browser product includes
                project-specific services and policies; review its current
                privacy information.</td
              >
              <td
                ><a href="https://brave.com/shields/">Shields</a><br /><a
                  href="https://brave.com/privacy/browser/">Privacy policy</a
                ><br /><a
                  href="https://github.com/brave/brave-browser/blob/master/LICENSE"
                  >Source license</a
                ></td
              >
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Cromite</th>
              <td
                >Community-maintained Chromium fork with Android builds; consult
                its current release notes for supported device architectures and
                Android versions.</td
              >
              <td
                >Built-in ad blocking and privacy-focused Chromium changes, as
                described in its project documentation.</td
              >
              <td
                >It is a separate browser distribution to install and keep
                updated. Review the project’s build, update, compatibility, and
                licensing notes for the exact release you use.</td
              >
              <td
                ><a href="https://github.com/qwez-dev/cromite"
                  >Project and release notes</a
                ><br /><a
                  href="https://github.com/qwez-dev/cromite/blob/master/LICENSE"
                  >GPL 3.0</a
                ></td
              >
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Vivaldi</th>
              <td
                >Vivaldi Browser for Android is a Chromium-based browser
                distributed by Vivaldi.</td
              >
              <td
                >Its Android help documents tracker and ad blocking, tab tools,
                and interface customization such as themes.</td
              >
              <td
                >Vivaldi is proprietary software. Its vendor documents that the
                browser is not wholly open source and that licensing varies
                across Chromium modifications and third-party components.</td
              >
              <td
                ><a href="https://help.vivaldi.com/android/">Android help</a><br
                /><a
                  href="https://help.vivaldi.com/android/android-privacy/android-tracker-and-ad-blocker/"
                  >Tracker and ad blocker</a
                ><br /><a
                  href="https://help.vivaldi.com/android/android-appearance/themes-on-android/"
                  >Themes</a
                ><br /><a
                  href="https://help.vivaldi.com/desktop/privacy/is-vivaldi-open-source/"
                  >Open-source and license notes</a
                ></td
              >
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="comparison-note">
        Comparison checked 31 October 2026 against the linked project
        documentation. Feature descriptions summarize those sources; they do not
        assess privacy practices, security, speed, or quality.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section comparison-guidance" aria-labelledby="fit-heading">
      <div class="shell comparison-guidance-grid">
        <div>
          <p class="kicker">Finding a fit</p>
          <h2 id="fit-heading">Start with the way you browse.</h2>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p>
            Solipsism may suit someone who wants an Android browser organized
            around a configurable side rail and built-in controls, and who is
            comfortable with WebView’s platform limits. Firefox may suit someone
            who wants Mozilla’s Android add-on catalog. Brave documents
            integrated Shields controls; Vivaldi emphasizes browser
            customization. Cromite offers a Chromium fork for users willing to
            manage a separate build and release channel.
          </p>
          <p>
            Compare each browser’s current permissions, update process, privacy
            information, compatibility, and license before installing. No
            feature list can establish that one browser is safer or more private
            for every person.
          </p>
          <div class="link-cluster">
            <a href={`${base}docs/`}>Solipsism technical guide</a>
            <a href={`${base}guides/getting-started/`}>Install guide</a>
            <a href="https://github.com/Kenneth-Cho-InfoSec/Solipsism/issues"
              >Ask a project question</a
            >
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>
